<comment>Resolved by WG3:ZSH-124, reported by WG3:SIA-046R1</comment>
<PP id="PPfrm009" number="FRM-009" severity="minor" realm="editorial">

<reference>
P01, SQL/Framework, <extref part="01" ref="frm_relationships_clauses"/>
</reference>


<source>
WG3:DRS-061/H2-2002-___
</source>


<problem>

<para>
<extref part="01" ref="frm_relationships_clauses"/>,
first paragraph is:
</para>

<slist>

<item>Where a Clause (other than Clause 1, "Scope",
and Clause 2, "Normative references"), Subclause, or Annex in any
incremental part of ISO/IEC 9075 has a name identical to a Clause,
Subclause, or Annex in ISO/IEC 9075-1, ISO/IEC 9075-2, ISO/IEC 9075-3,
ISO/IEC 9075-10, or ISO/IEC 9075-11, unless the incremental part is itself
ISO/IEC 9075-3, ISO/IEC 9075-10, or ISO/IEC 9075-11, it supplements the
Clause, Subclause, or Annex, respectively, in ISO/IEC 9075-1, ISO/IEC
9075-2, ISO/IEC 9075-3, ISO/IEC 9075-10, or ISO/IEC 9075-11, regardless of
whether or not the number or letter of the Clause, Subclause, or Annex
corresponds. It typically does so by adding or replacing paragraphs,
Format items, or Rules.
</item>

</slist>

<para>
Since Framework already contains statements about other parts of ISO/IEC
9075, there is no reason to exclude further additions. This point can be
demonstrated by referring to [FrameWD],
<extref part="01" ref="frm_notterminated"/>, which is:
</para>

<slist>

<item>This Subclause is modified by <extref part="04" ref="psm_notterminated"/>, 
in ISO/IEC 9075-4. 

<para>
The General Rules of the following Subclauses are not terminated where 
an exception condition is raised:
</para>

<ulist>

<item>Subclause 10.4, "<BNF name="routine invocation"/>", in ISO/IEC 9075-2. 
</item>

<item>Subclause 13.5, "<BNF name="SQL procedure statement"/>", in ISO/IEC 9075-2. 
</item>

<item>Subclause 21.1, "<BNF name="direct SQL statement"/>", in ISO/IEC 9075-2. 
</item>

</ulist>

</item>

</slist>

<para>
If there is any reason to include here references to subclauses of Part 2,
then it is hard to imagine why references to subclauses of Part 4 should
not also be here.  If there is a reason, then surely it applies with equal
force to the existing references to subclauses of Part 2. 
</para>

</problem>

</PP>


